WEBVTT
NOTE how-is-a-silicon-wafer-made - timed from measured narration audio

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.783
Semiconductor-grade silicon reaches
99.9999999 percent purity — nine nines.

00:00:05.783 --> 00:00:12.261
That figure comes from SEMI standards and
represents fewer than one foreign atom per

00:00:12.261 --> 00:00:13.957
billion silicon atoms.

00:00:13.957 --> 00:00:17.031
But nine-nines silicon does not exist in
nature.

00:00:17.031 --> 00:00:22.026
Every wafer starts as quartzite sand,
which is only about ninety-eight percent

00:00:22.026 --> 00:00:23.051
silicon dioxide.

00:00:23.051 --> 00:00:26.907
The gap between ninety-eight and nine
nines is not a simple filter.

00:00:26.907 --> 00:00:30.838
It is a sequence of transformations, each
with its own thermal budget and

00:00:30.838 --> 00:00:31.915
contamination limit.

00:00:31.915 --> 00:00:34.011
How is a silicon wafer made?

00:00:34.011 --> 00:00:36.435
The first stage is carbothermic reduction.

00:00:36.435 --> 00:00:41.109
Quartzite — high-purity quartz sand — is
loaded into a submerged arc furnace with

00:00:41.109 --> 00:00:43.360
carbon sources like coal or wood chips.

00:00:43.360 --> 00:00:46.528
The furnace reaches 2000 degrees Celsius.

00:00:46.528 --> 00:00:52.786
At that temperature, carbon strips oxygen
from silicon dioxide, leaving elemental

00:00:52.786 --> 00:00:55.259
silicon and carbon monoxide gas.

00:00:55.259 --> 00:01:00.439
The product is metallurgical-grade
silicon, about ninety-eight to ninety-nine

00:01:00.439 --> 00:01:01.313
percent pure.

00:01:01.313 --> 00:01:06.359
USGS Minerals Yearbook documents this
process as the standard route for all

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:07.637
industrial silicon.

00:01:07.637 --> 00:01:12.704
The arc furnace step removes oxygen but
leaves metallic impurities — iron,

00:01:12.704 --> 00:01:17.701
aluminum, calcium — at levels measured in
thousands of parts per million.

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:22.712
The molten silicon is tapped from the
furnace and cast into blocks that cool to

00:01:22.712 --> 00:01:24.171
a gray, metallic solid.

00:01:24.171 --> 00:01:29.199
This material is conductive enough for
aluminum alloys and solar cells, but not

00:01:29.199 --> 00:01:30.853
for semiconductor devices.

00:01:30.853 --> 00:01:35.523
Purification to semiconductor grade uses
the Siemens process, named after the

00:01:35.523 --> 00:01:38.859
company that commercialized it in the
nineteen-fifties.

00:01:38.859 --> 00:01:43.842
Metallurgical-grade silicon reacts with
hydrogen chloride gas at 300 degrees

00:01:43.842 --> 00:01:49.349
Celsius, forming trichlorosilane, a liquid
that boils at thirty-two degrees Celsius.

00:01:49.349 --> 00:01:51.809
Trichlorosilane is distilled repeatedly.

00:01:51.809 --> 00:01:56.114
Distillation separates molecules by
boiling point, and impurities have

00:01:56.114 --> 00:01:59.373
different boiling points than
trichlorosilane itself.

00:01:59.373 --> 00:02:04.448
After multiple distillation cycles, the
trichlorosilane reaches high purity.

00:02:04.448 --> 00:02:09.790
It is then fed into a reactor where it
contacts electrically heated silicon rods

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.392
at 1100 degrees Celsius.

00:02:11.392 --> 00:02:16.709
Hydrogen gas reduces the trichlorosilane
back to elemental silicon, which deposits

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:19.237
on the rods as polycrystalline silicon.

00:02:19.237 --> 00:02:24.153
The Siemens process is documented in
detail by SEMI International Standards,

00:02:24.153 --> 00:02:28.811
which defines the purity specifications
for semiconductor manufacturing.

00:02:28.811 --> 00:02:33.710
The polysilicon that emerges measures
nine-nines purity or better.

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:39.425
Each reactor cycle deposits tens to
hundreds of kilograms of polysilicon over

00:02:39.425 --> 00:02:40.390
several days.

00:02:40.390 --> 00:02:45.957
The rods grow from pencil thickness to
diameters exceeding ten centimeters.

00:02:45.957 --> 00:02:51.239
Polysilicon is polycrystalline — it
contains millions of randomly oriented

00:02:51.239 --> 00:02:52.309
crystal grains.

00:02:52.309 --> 00:02:57.050
Semiconductor devices require
single-crystal material, where every atom

00:02:57.050 --> 00:02:59.120
sits in one continuous lattice.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:05.365
The Czochralski method, developed in 1916
and adapted for silicon in 1950, produces

00:03:05.365 --> 00:03:06.869
that single crystal.

00:03:06.869 --> 00:03:12.215
Polysilicon chunks are melted in a quartz
crucible at 1414 degrees Celsius,

00:03:12.215 --> 00:03:13.925
silicon's melting point.

00:03:13.925 --> 00:03:18.851
A seed crystal — a small rod of
single-crystal silicon with a specific

00:03:18.851 --> 00:03:23.424
orientation — is lowered until it just
contacts the melt surface.

00:03:23.424 --> 00:03:28.624
The seed rotates slowly and is pulled
upward at a rate of millimeters per

00:03:28.624 --> 00:03:34.464
Silicon atoms from the melt attach to the
seed and adopt its crystal orientation.

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:39.739
The ingot grows as a cylinder. Diameter is
controlled by pull rate and temperature.

00:03:39.739 --> 00:03:45.546
Modern Czochralski pullers produce ingots
200 millimeters or 300 millimeters in

00:03:45.546 --> 00:03:48.192
diameter and one to two meters long.

00:03:48.192 --> 00:03:52.806
NIST and MIT Department of Materials
Science and Engineering both describe

00:03:52.806 --> 00:03:57.296
Czochralski growth as the dominant
production method for silicon ingots.

00:03:57.296 --> 00:04:00.272
The entire pull takes twenty to thirty
hours.

00:04:00.272 --> 00:04:05.826
The process occurs in an inert atmosphere,
typically argon, to prevent oxidation and

00:04:05.826 --> 00:04:06.752
contamination.

00:04:06.752 --> 00:04:12.248
Operators monitor diameter optically in
real time and adjust heater power and pull

00:04:12.248 --> 00:04:14.192
speed to maintain the target.

00:04:14.192 --> 00:04:20.280
I wrote 'nine-nines pure' like purity was
a certificate stamped once. It isn't.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.135
Every step after purification can
reintroduce contamination, so I rewrote it

00:04:25.135 --> 00:04:28.712
as a chain of measurements instead of an
achieved state.

00:04:28.712 --> 00:04:34.573
Pure silicon is a poor conductor.
Controlled impurities — dopants — make it

00:04:34.573 --> 00:04:38.897
Boron creates p-type silicon, with
positive charge carriers.

00:04:38.897 --> 00:04:44.373
Phosphorus or arsenic creates n-type
silicon, with negative charge carriers.

00:04:44.373 --> 00:04:49.373
Dopants are added to the Czochralski melt
in precise amounts, typically parts per

00:04:49.373 --> 00:04:51.163
million or parts per billion.

00:04:51.163 --> 00:04:55.925
The dopant concentration determines the
wafer's resistivity, measured in

00:04:55.925 --> 00:04:56.983
ohm-centimeters.

00:04:56.983 --> 00:05:01.547
SEMI standards specify resistivity ranges
for different applications.

00:05:01.547 --> 00:05:06.977
The dopant distributes unevenly along the
ingot because its solubility differs

00:05:06.977 --> 00:05:09.275
between solid and liquid silicon.

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:14.059
This is called segregation, and it is
quantified by the segregation coefficient.

00:05:14.059 --> 00:05:19.240
For boron in silicon, the coefficient is
about 0.8, meaning boron concentrates

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.701
slightly in the solid.

00:05:20.701 --> 00:05:25.645
For phosphorus, it is about 0.35, meaning
phosphorus stays in the melt.

00:05:25.645 --> 00:05:30.166
Ingot producers measure resistivity at
multiple points and cut the ingot into

00:05:30.166 --> 00:05:31.987
zones of acceptable uniformity.

00:05:31.987 --> 00:05:36.141
The first-to-freeze material has lower
dopant concentration than the

00:05:36.141 --> 00:05:37.607
last-to-freeze material.

00:05:37.607 --> 00:05:41.944
This variation is predictable and
accounted for in production planning.

00:05:41.944 --> 00:05:48.453
The cylindrical ingot is ground to a
precise diameter — 200.0 millimeters or

00:05:48.453 --> 00:05:52.051
300.0 millimeters, within tens of microns.

00:05:52.051 --> 00:05:56.451
A flat or notch is ground along one side
to mark crystal orientation.

00:05:56.451 --> 00:05:58.199
The ingot is then sliced.

00:05:58.199 --> 00:06:03.863
Multi-wire saws use hundreds of parallel
steel wires under tension, moving at ten

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.821
to twenty meters per second.

00:06:05.821 --> 00:06:10.617
An abrasive slurry — typically silicon
carbide or diamond particles suspended in

00:06:10.617 --> 00:06:12.835
oil or glycol — flows over the wires.

00:06:12.835 --> 00:06:16.740
The abrasive does the cutting; the wire is
the vehicle.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:22.207
A single pass through a 300-millimeter
ingot can yield several hundred wafers

00:06:22.207 --> 00:06:23.272
simultaneously.

00:06:23.272 --> 00:06:30.854
Finished wafer thickness is 775 microns
for 200-millimeter wafers and 925 microns

00:06:30.854 --> 00:06:35.160
for 300-millimeter wafers, per SEMI
standards.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:40.129
The sawing step removes about half the
ingot as kerf loss — the material turned

00:06:40.129 --> 00:06:41.576
to slurry between cuts.

00:06:41.576 --> 00:06:46.576
Wire tension, speed and slurry composition
are tuned to minimize subsurface damage

00:06:46.576 --> 00:06:48.344
while maintaining throughput.

00:06:48.344 --> 00:06:53.885
Each wafer emerges with saw marks and a
damaged surface layer several microns

00:06:53.885 --> 00:06:55.562
As-sawn wafers are rough.

00:06:55.562 --> 00:07:01.060
Lapping uses a rotating plate with loose
abrasive to remove saw damage and flatten

00:07:01.060 --> 00:07:01.731
the wafer.

00:07:01.731 --> 00:07:06.036
The lapped surface has a roughness around
800 nanometers RMS.

00:07:06.036 --> 00:07:11.611
Chemical etching removes the damaged
subsurface layer left by lapping, reducing

00:07:11.611 --> 00:07:14.152
roughness to about fifty nanometers.

00:07:14.152 --> 00:07:17.610
The final step is chemical-mechanical
polishing, or CMP.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:22.487
The wafer is pressed against a rotating
pad while a slurry containing nanoscale

00:07:22.487 --> 00:07:26.253
abrasive particles and reactive chemicals
flows between them.

00:07:26.253 --> 00:07:30.609
The chemical component weakens silicon
bonds; the mechanical component removes

00:07:30.609 --> 00:07:31.837
the weakened material.

00:07:31.837 --> 00:07:37.616
Polishing continues until the surface
reaches a roughness below 0.1 nanometers

00:07:37.616 --> 00:07:40.579
RMS — effectively atomic-scale flatness.

00:07:40.579 --> 00:07:45.511
NIST and SEMI both publish metrology
standards for wafer surface finish.

00:07:45.511 --> 00:07:50.717
The polished wafer is cleaned in ultrapure
water and packaged in a cleanroom

00:07:50.717 --> 00:07:51.539
environment.

00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:56.573
Surface inspection uses laser scattering
and atomic force microscopy to detect

00:07:56.573 --> 00:07:59.736
particles and defects down to tens of
nanometers.

00:07:59.736 --> 00:08:05.575
Only wafers meeting flatness, roughness
and defect specifications ship to device

00:08:05.575 --> 00:08:06.451
fabricators.

00:08:06.451 --> 00:08:11.644
The seed crystal used in Czochralski
pulling has a specific crystallographic

00:08:11.644 --> 00:08:15.949
orientation, typically 100 or 111,
described by Miller indices.

00:08:15.949 --> 00:08:20.808
These indices describe which atomic plane
is parallel to the wafer surface.

00:08:20.808 --> 00:08:25.473
Orientation affects mechanical properties
— silicon cleaves more easily along

00:08:25.473 --> 00:08:29.593
certain planes — and electrical
properties, because carrier mobility

00:08:29.593 --> 00:08:30.925
varies with direction.

00:08:30.925 --> 00:08:35.914
The 100 orientation is standard for most
logic and memory devices.

00:08:35.914 --> 00:08:40.147
The 111 orientation is sometimes used for
power devices.

00:08:40.147 --> 00:08:45.208
The flat or notch ground onto the ingot
marks the orientation so that device

00:08:45.208 --> 00:08:50.403
fabricators can align their lithography
and etch steps to the crystal lattice.

00:08:50.403 --> 00:08:55.403
ASM International and MIT Department of
Materials Science and Engineering both

00:08:55.403 --> 00:09:00.339
document the relationship between crystal
orientation and device performance.

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:05.381
Orientation is not adjusted after the seed
is chosen; it propagates through the

00:09:05.381 --> 00:09:06.211
entire ingot.

00:09:06.211 --> 00:09:10.325
Purity is not a single number frozen at
the Siemens step.

00:09:10.325 --> 00:09:14.728
Every thermal process after purification
risks contamination.

00:09:14.728 --> 00:09:19.799
The quartz crucible in Czochralski growth
dissolves slightly, adding oxygen to the

00:09:19.799 --> 00:09:23.139
melt at levels around ten to twenty parts
per million.

00:09:23.139 --> 00:09:28.737
Oxygen is sometimes intentional — it
improves mechanical strength — but it is

00:09:28.737 --> 00:09:30.045
still an impurity.

00:09:30.045 --> 00:09:32.991
Carbon enters from graphite furnace
components.

00:09:32.991 --> 00:09:36.813
Metals can diffuse from tooling during
slicing and polishing.

00:09:36.813 --> 00:09:41.728
Each wafer is tested for resistivity,
which reflects dopant concentration, and

00:09:41.728 --> 00:09:46.013
for minority carrier lifetime, which
reflects deep-level impurities.

00:09:46.013 --> 00:09:50.929
These are electrical measurements, not
direct chemical assays, but they correlate

00:09:50.929 --> 00:09:52.386
with device performance.

00:09:52.386 --> 00:09:56.635
The published specifications are limits,
not guarantees of uniformity.

00:09:56.635 --> 00:10:01.908
The four-week figure for total production
time is an estimate that depends on ingot

00:10:01.908 --> 00:10:07.053
diameter, facility scheduling and whether
all steps occur at one site or multiple

00:10:07.053 --> 00:10:07.435
sites.

00:10:07.435 --> 00:10:11.521
Czochralski pulling alone takes one to two
days per ingot.

00:10:11.521 --> 00:10:15.536
Slicing, lapping, etching and polishing
add several days.

00:10:15.536 --> 00:10:20.788
The Siemens process operates continuously
but polysilicon accumulates over days to

00:10:20.788 --> 00:10:23.093
weeks before a reactor is harvested.

00:10:23.093 --> 00:10:27.996
No single public source tracks end-to-end
cycle time across all producers.

00:10:27.996 --> 00:10:33.032
The figure represents typical industry
practice as described in SEMI and ASM

00:10:33.032 --> 00:10:36.411
International references, not a measured
universal.

00:10:36.411 --> 00:10:38.885
A silicon wafer is not refined from
silicon.

00:10:38.885 --> 00:10:43.553
It is built from it, through a sequence of
subtractive and additive steps that each

00:10:43.553 --> 00:10:47.996
operate at a different temperature and
each introduce a different contamination

00:10:47.996 --> 00:10:48.277
risk.

00:10:48.277 --> 00:10:52.557
The nine-nines purity is the result of
that sequence, not a property of the

00:10:52.557 --> 00:10:53.584
starting material.
